<section class="nc-guide hub-guide" id="guide">
  <div class="prose">
    <h2>How the 3 health tools fit together</h2>
    <p>Each calculator in this section answers a different question, and they are most useful read together rather than alone.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Tool</th><th>What you need</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Is my weight in a typical range for my height?</td><td><a href="../calculators/bmi.html">BMI calculator</a></td><td>Height and weight</td></tr>
      <tr><td>How much of my weight is fat?</td><td><a href="../calculators/body-fat.html">Body-fat estimate</a></td><td>Height and a tape measure</td></tr>
      <tr><td>How much energy do I use in a day?</td><td><a href="../calculators/tdee.html">TDEE calculator</a></td><td>Age, sex, height, weight, activity level</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>A typical sequence is to start with BMI as a quick screen, use the body-fat estimate if BMI seems off for your build (common for muscular people), and use TDEE to understand how much food energy your current routine requires. The <a href="body/">body measurements</a> and <a href="nutrition/">nutrition and energy</a> pages explain each one in more depth.</p>

    <h2>What every result here has in common</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>They are population formulas.</strong> Each equation describes the average person with your inputs. Your own body can differ, sometimes by a lot.</li>
      <li><strong>Trends beat single readings.</strong> Measuring the same way every few weeks tells you more than any one result.</li>
      <li><strong>They are not diagnoses.</strong> None of these tools can account for medications, pregnancy, medical conditions, or family history. They are written for adults; children and teens are assessed with age-specific growth charts.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Your entries are calculated in your browser and are not sent to NumberCove. If a result worries you, or if you have a history of disordered eating, a physician or registered dietitian is the right next step rather than another calculator.</p>
  </div>
</section>
